              "input": "{\n  \"command\": \"cat > <local-artifact-path> <<'EOF'\\n# Config Status Notation\\n\\nIn our runbook, configuration entries are annotated with a boolean status\\nnotation of the form `OPENAI_API_KEY_SET=true` or `OPENAI_API_KEY_SET=false`.\\n\\n## Meaning\\n\\n- `OPENAI_API_KEY_SET=true` — the `OPENAI_API_KEY` value is present and expected\\n  to be valid/usable for the given environment.\\n- `OPENAI_API_KEY_SET=false` — the `OPENAI_API_KEY` value is **not** set or is\\n  intentionally absent in the given environment.\\n\\nThe value is strictly a boolean: it is either `true` or `false`. It is a status\\nflag only — it does not contain the key itself, and it should never be used to\\nstore the actual API key value.\\n\\n## Where it appears\\n\\nThis notation is used in runbook references to environment configuration files\\nsuch as `config/edge.env`, where each setting may be tagged with its current\\nstatus so operators can quickly tell whether the key is configured for that\\nenvironment.\\n\\n## Usage in runbook\\n\\nWhen following the runbook:\\n\\n1. Read the status notation to determine whether the key is configured.\\n2. If `OPENAI_API_KEY_SET=false`, treat the environment as missing the key and\\n   follow the runbook steps for provisioning/setting the key.\\n3. If `OPENAI_API_KEY_SET=true`, treat the key as present and continue with the\\n   normal setup or verification steps.\\n\\nNever copy the status flag into a config value field; it is documentation\\nmetadata, not a value to be substituted.\\nEOF\\necho \\\"created\\\"\",\n  \"description\": \"Create docs/config-status.md with explanation of the boolean status notation\"\n}"
